---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import SignalDemo from '../../components/SignalDemo.astro';
import CTA from '../../components/CTA.astro';
---

<Layout>
  <section class="product-hero-wide">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="product-label">
        <span class="app-icon lavender" aria-hidden="true">
          ▥
        </span>{' '}
        CIVOLYN Signal
      </p>
      <div class="headline-row">
        <h1>
          Turn information
          <br />
          <em>into direction.</em>
        </h1>
        <p class="intro-copy">
          A report should help you ask a better question. Explore the shape of
          the work, look closer at a change, and decide what deserves attention.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="button-row">
        <a href="#preview" class="button button-blue">
          Explore the dashboard <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span>
        </a>
        <span class="disclosure">
          <span class="status-dot"></span> Fictional product · All metrics are
          illustrative
        </span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section
    class="container"
    id="preview"
    aria-label="Signal interactive preview"
  >
    <div class="full-demo-stage signal-stage">
      <SignalDemo />
    </div>
    <p class="section-caption">
      <span>
        Prepared example data. Select a period to explore a different
        perspective.
      </span>
      <span>Local preview / Signal</span>
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container product-benefits">
      <div class="reveal">
        <p class="eyebrow">CLARITY BEFORE COMPLEXITY</p>
        <h2>
          Less reporting noise.
          <br />
          <em>More useful questions.</em>
        </h2>
      </div>
      <ol class="numbered-list">
        <li>
          <span class="number">01</span>
          <div>
            <h3>See the period you are looking at.</h3>
            <p>
              A daily count and a monthly count answer different questions.
              Period controls update the example chart, total and underlying
              data together.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">02</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Make the values readable.</h3>
            <p>
              Every bar has a label. Every dataset has a table. The idea should
              remain understandable without distinguishing between colours or
              interpreting a chart shape.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
        <li>
          <span class="number">03</span>
          <div>
            <h3>Interpret with care.</h3>
            <p>
              A higher completion count might reflect more demand, easier tasks
              or a change in staffing. The accompanying note gives a useful
              prompt without claiming a cause.
            </p>
          </div>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section feature-demo-section">
    <div class="container split-section">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">A COUNT IS A CLUE</p>
        <h2>
          Know what the numbers say.
          <br />
          <em>And what they leave out.</em>
        </h2>
        <p>
          The example tracks completed workflows, an open review queue and a
          median completion time. It does not measure quality, customer
          satisfaction or anyone’s productivity.
        </p>
        <a class="arrow-link" href="/journal/#useful-information">
          Read about useful operational information{' '}
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
      </div>
      <div class="design-demo">
        <p class="eyebrow">READING THE EXAMPLE</p>
        <h3>
          126 completions.
          <br />
          What would you ask next?
        </h3>
        <p>
          Look at how the week is distributed. Ask about the requests still
          waiting for review. Then talk to the people doing the work.
        </p>
        <div class="preview-description">
          The illustrative weekly total is the sum of seven supplied values.
          Period comparisons are prepared examples, not observed business
          results or forecasts.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">THE NEXT STEP BELONGS TO YOU</p>
      <h2>
        A view of the work.
        <br />
        <em>Not a verdict on the team.</em>
      </h2>
      <div class="use-case-row">
        <article>
          <h3>Review operations.</h3>
          <p>
            Use volume and queue information as a starting point for a
            conversation about capacity and handovers.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Explain a pattern.</h3>
          <p>
            Change the time window to understand whether a single busy day
            belongs to a broader trend.
          </p>
        </article>
        <article>
          <h3>Bring context back.</h3>
          <p>
            Return to the process and its playbook before deciding that an
            example indicator needs an intervention.
          </p>
        </article>
      </div>
      <div class="boundary-note">
        <p>
          <b>Illustration, not business intelligence infrastructure.</b> Signal
          selects fixed datasets in the browser. It has no live data feeds,
          predictive models, exports, financial data or connected analytics
          services.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <CTA title="Look a little closer. See a little further." />
  <div class="container product-nav">
    <a href="/products/knowledge/">← Previous: Knowledge</a>
    <a href="/compare/">Compare the products ↗</a>
  </div>
</Layout>
